From: ReArc Authors
Subject: [PATCH] P-TAB-001 dark mode for websites: the host says which pages are dark, Blink's Auto Dark Mode does it (ABI 1.73, bridge 0.85.0)

Darni had no dark mode for websites. A site with a dark theme of its own
followed the appearance of macOS, and a site without one stayed white. This
patch lets the host say, per profile, that pages are dark and which hosts
differ, and makes a dark page use Chromium's own means: the dark colour
scheme for a site that has one, and Blink's Auto Dark Mode (forced
darkening) for one that has none.

What the host says.
- ReArcProfileSetWebsiteDarkMode(profile_id, dark, dark_hosts_json,
  light_hosts_json): whether the profile's pages are dark, and two lists of
  hosts that are dark or light whatever `dark` says. A host is matched
  whole. Up to 10,000 hosts a list; a host in both lists, a string that is
  not a host or malformed JSON is invalid_argument and changes nothing. An
  off-the-record profile is invalid_argument: a private window follows its
  profile.
- ReArcTabGetWebsiteDarkMode(tab_id, &dark): what the tab holds now.
- Feature "website_dark_mode".

What the engine does with it.
- rearc::WebsiteDarkModeApplies(contents) answers for a page: a bridge tab
  (never a DevTools frontend, an extension's popup or side panel, or any
  other contents the engine makes for itself) whose main frame has committed
  an http or https address, by that address's host. A local file, about:blank,
  the engine's own pages and error pages are never dark by it.
- ChromeContentBrowserClient asks it in the three places a tab's colour
  preferences are worked out:
  GetPreferredColorScheme (so OverrideWebPreferences,
  OverrideWebPreferencesAfterNavigation and
  WebPreferencesNeedUpdateForColorRelatedStateChanges agree): a dark page
  prefers the dark scheme and its root scrollbar is dark;
  OverrideWebPreferences: force_dark_mode_enabled for such a page;
  OverrideWebPreferencesAfterNavigation: the same again for the page that has
  just committed, and the profile's own preference back for a page that is
  not dark. That is the "rule read at navigation".
- When the host says something new, every tab of the profile and of its
  private windows works its preferences out again
  (WebContents::OnWebPreferencesChanged). The renderers get what changed and
  restyle: open tabs follow without a reload.
- Nothing is persisted. The state hangs on the Profile object
  (SupportsUserData) and the host says it again at the next profile load. No
  preference, no content setting, nothing in Local State.
- No feature flag is switched on: blink::features::kForceWebContentsDarkMode
  only changes the default of the profile preference
  webkit.webprefs.force_dark_mode_enabled, which stays false and is not
  written. Blink reads the web preference alone.

What Chromium then does, unchanged (Chromium 156; stated because the brief
asked whether forced darkening also sets prefers-color-scheme):
- (prefers-color-scheme: dark) matches in a dark page. The comment on
  StyleEngine::preferred_color_scheme_ still says a force-darkened page is
  told it prefers light; the code of 156 does not do that
  (StyleEngine::UpdateColorScheme takes the setting as it is, and the host
  test reads "matches" from the page). So a site that
  styles its dark theme by media query gets it, and the darkening then finds
  little that is still light.
- A page that declares a dark scheme (color-scheme "dark" or "light dark",
  by meta tag or on its root) is drawn in it and is not darkened
  (ComputedStyleBuilder::SetUsedColorScheme: dark, not forced).
- A page that declares nothing, or "light", is darkened as it is painted:
  backgrounds brighter than 205 and text darker than 150 (of 255) are
  inverted in lightness, what is already dark stays
  (dark_mode_settings_builder.cc). Photographs and videos are not touched;
  an image that is drawn small enough to be an icon, or thin enough to be a
  separator, is classified and adapted when it would vanish on dark
  (DarkModeFilter::ShouldApplyFilterToImage).
- The page's computed styles are its own; only the CSS system colours
  change (Canvas becomes #121212), which is how a page can tell.
- color-scheme "only light" opts a page or an element out.

Known limits.
- The preferences reach a renderer after the navigation has committed. A
  page whose state differs from the page before it (the first page of a new
  tab; a host on one of the lists) can paint a first frame in the old state.
  With "dark" for the whole profile and no list, same-tab navigations do not
  change state and show nothing of it.
- A host is matched whole, as zoom is: example.com and www.example.com are
  two sites.

State. Nothing is persisted by the engine. An engine without this patch
does not export the two functions, and a host that looks them up finds none.

ABI 1.73: two functions, no struct, no callback type. 1.72 is tab/104's
(local passkeys), which landed on the checkout after this patch.

Tests: engine/tests/c_abi_dark_mode_host.mm (run_c_abi.py dark_mode), with
a window off every screen and no input: a page with no dark theme is white
while nothing is said (drawn 255,255,255); when the profile goes dark the
same document, not reloaded, is drawn 18,18,18, its computed Canvas is
18,18,18, (prefers-color-scheme: dark) matches and its body's computed
background is still white; its host on the light list makes it white again
without a reload; a navigation to another host is dark; dark for one host
only is white on the other and dark after navigating back; a page with its
own dark scheme is drawn in its own colour (16,32,48); a page that says
"only light" stays white; a private window's tab follows its profile; ten
refusals change nothing. engine/tests/check_abi173_imports.py: layouts kept,
two functions added, gated Swift adoption typechecks. The shell's self-test
step "dark mode" (ChromiumSelfTest.websiteDarkModeChecks) drives the same
through Settings' setting and the site controls row.

Type-checked with the tree's clang (out/ReArc-codecs-arm64 flags, Chromium's
plugins, -fsyntax-only; rearc_website_dark_mode.cc and
chrome_content_browser_client.cc), then built in out/ReArc-codecs-arm64 on
2026-11-25: overlay 30c30a1c8d on a3b96b217d (tab/103).

Run on that build with tab/103's exports (577e70cef2) and tab/104
(faf0faf7a4) on top (run_c_abi.py dark_mode, REARC_CHROMIUM_OUT the release
out dir, nothing on screen, bridge 0.86.0): every step above passed; the
network audit shows loopback only. Not run: the shell's self-test step,
which needs the screen.

Touches: chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc (three hunks and an
include), chrome/browser/BUILD.gn, chrome/app/framework.{exports,order}, and
the bridge's rearc_engine_abi.{h,mm}, rearc_engine_entry_mac.mm and the new
rearc_website_dark_mode.{h,cc}.

Applies after tab/103 (its context is ABI 1.71, bridge 0.83.0) and before
tab/104, as the overlay has them.

Last rebased: 2026-11-25 onto Chromium 156.0.8078.114 (overlay a3b96b217d).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>

