+// Dark mode for websites (website_dark_mode, ABI 1.73, tab/105). The host
+// says, per profile, whether pages are dark and which hosts differ; the
+// engine reads that for a tab's page at every main frame navigation and
+// whenever the host says something new, and open tabs follow without a
+// reload. A page that is dark gets two of Chromium's web preferences:
+// preferred_color_scheme dark and force_dark_mode_enabled (Blink's Auto Dark
+// Mode). Chromium then does this, and the bridge changes none of it:
+// - (prefers-color-scheme: dark) matches, whatever the appearance of macOS
+//   is, so a page's own dark styles apply.
+// - A page that says it has a dark scheme (color-scheme "dark" or "light
+//   dark", by <meta name="color-scheme"> or on its root element) is drawn in
+//   it and is not darkened.
+// - A page that says nothing, or only "light", is darkened as it is painted:
+//   light backgrounds become dark and dark text light. What is already dark
+//   stays. Photographs and videos are left alone; small images that read as
+//   icons or separators are adapted. The page's computed styles do not
+//   change, except the CSS system colours (Canvas, CanvasText).
+// - color-scheme "only light" opts a page or an element out.
+// It covers the pages of bridge tabs on http and https addresses: never
+// DevTools, an extension's pages, the engine's own pages, a local file or an
+// error page. Frames follow their tab's page. A page that is not dark is as
+// before: its scheme follows the appearance of macOS and nothing is darkened.
+//
+// dark: the profile's pages are dark. dark_hosts_json, light_hosts_json: JSON
+// arrays of at most 10,000 hosts each, lower-case ASCII (punycode) as in a
+// URL's host, without scheme, port or path; null is an empty array. A page
+// whose host is in the light list is never dark, one in the dark list always
+// is, any other follows `dark`. A host is matched whole: "example.com" does
+// not cover "www.example.com". A host in both lists, a string that is not a
+// host, a longer list or malformed JSON: invalid_argument, and nothing
+// changes. profile_id is never an off-the-record profile (invalid_argument):
+// private windows follow their profile. The engine keeps this in memory with
+// the profile and writes nothing: the host is the source of truth and says
+// it again at every profile load. A profile it has said nothing about has no
+// dark pages.
+REARC_ENGINE_EXPORT ReArcStatus ReArcProfileSetWebsiteDarkMode(
+    uint64_t profile_id, bool dark, const char* dark_hosts_json,
+    const char* light_hosts_json);
+// Whether the tab's page is dark by the above now: both web preferences as
+// the tab holds them. dark null: invalid_argument.
+REARC_ENGINE_EXPORT ReArcStatus ReArcTabGetWebsiteDarkMode(uint64_t tab_id,
+                                                           bool* dark);
+
